*.tsbuildinfo
# Every .env variant, not just the two that were thought of. A file named
# .env.server or .env.production holds exactly the same kind of secret as .env,
# and the version that gets committed is always the one nobody added a line for.
.env*
!.env.example
.DS_Store
.agent-workspace/
# Database dumps and photo archives taken by bin/deploy.sh on the host.
.deploy-backups/
# Test artefacts: the suite's own upload directory and coverage output.
.test-uploads/
coverage/
# Copied from node_modules by apps/web/scripts/copy-zxing.mjs at build time.
apps/web/public/zxing_reader.wasm

# Push credentials.
#
# Two files, and only one of them is a secret — which is exactly why both are
# named here rather than left to judgement in a hurry.
#
# `google-services.json` is client config: it tells the app which Firebase
# project to ask for a token. It is shipped inside every APK, so it is not
# secret in the password sense — but it is account-identifying, it differs per
# environment, and a repo is the wrong place to pin one copy of it.
#
# The service-account key is a real secret. It authorises *sending* as this
# project, and it is uploaded once to EAS rather than living here at all.
google-services.json
apps/mobile/google-services.json
play-service-account.json
apps/mobile/play-service-account.json
*-firebase-adminsdk-*.json
GoogleService-Info.plist
apps/mobile/GoogleService-Info.plist

# The APNs signing key, in every place a browser or a hurried `mv` can drop it.
#
# Downloadable exactly once: Apple removes its own copy as the download starts,
# so a `.p8` that reaches a commit cannot be rotated by fetching a fresh one —
# the key has to be revoked and every consumer repointed at a new key id. Apple
# fixes the name as `AuthKey_<KEYID>.p8`, which is what makes the broad glob
# safe to write: nothing else in this repo is a `.p8`.
AuthKey_*.p8
*.p8

# The TikTok refresh token.
#
# Not a config file with a secret in it — the whole file IS the credential. It
# authorises posting video as @daysofarapp for a year, and unlike an API key
# there is no dashboard listing it to revoke a leaked copy from: recovery is
# revoking the app's access from the TikTok account's own settings, which kills
# every machine's token at once. Written 0600 by scripts/tiktok/lib.mjs.
scripts/tiktok/.tokens.json
